{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480882-does-a-curse-end-if-you-kill-him","id":1480882,"title":"Does a Curse End if you Kill him?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-05-12T16:18:48-07:00","id":1480882,"title":"Does a Curse End if you Kill him?","content":"So, I was in this metaphysical discussion with a friend of mine, thinking about whether a curse cast by a mage ends which his demise or not.<br /> What is your guy's opinion on the matter?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-12T17:22:42-07:00","id":1480898,"title":"","content":"I've always thought that laying a curse is sort of like birthing a child. You give it its start, put some of yourself into it and help establish its parameters, but it continues on independently without you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-12T17:26:25-07:00","id":1480900,"title":"","content":"Thanks, I was of the same opinion in the discussion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-12T17:56:07-07:00","id":1480911,"title":"","content":"It makes me think of the side-quest in Planescape: Torment to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KuuLWEjJ7PI\" target=\"_blank\">remove Reekwind's curse of stench</a>. In Mage, a character that can curse someone can probably also kill them. There are two advantages to a curse. First, it avoids the characters allies seeking vengeance for a dead companion. Second, the victim has to negotiate the curse's removal by the caster. I am also running a story with a curse.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thank you for passing time with me in conversation. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480242-horizon-s-wild-and-crazy-homebrew-rules-and-metaplot-choices\" target=\"_blank\">My Hacks</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26467-horizonparty2021","name":"HorizonParty2021","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26467_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-12T18:37:42-07:00","id":1480915,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HorizonParty2021</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480882-does-a-curse-end-if-you-kill-him?p=1480911#post1480911\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It makes me think of the side-quest in Planescape: Torment to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KuuLWEjJ7PI\" target=\"_blank\">remove Reekwind's curse of stench</a>. In Mage, a character that can curse someone can probably also kill them. There are two advantages to a curse. First, it avoids the characters allies seeking vengeance for a dead companion. Second, the victim has to negotiate the curse's removal by the caster. I am also running a story with a curse.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yes, the fact that curses can be used as a bargaining chip is probably its greatest advantage. It's also interesting from a story point of view. Do you mind sharing the details of your story?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-12T22:43:40-07:00","id":1480932,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480882-does-a-curse-end-if-you-kill-him#post1480882\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, I was in this metaphysical discussion with a friend of mine, thinking about whether a curse cast by a mage ends which his demise or not.<br /> What is your guy's opinion on the matter?</div> </div> </div> </div>The rules are extremely bad at codifying when a spell is something a mage is actively powering and would end when the mages dies, and when a spell is a distinct event where the consequences continue logically from whatever the spell started.<br /> <br /> Sometimes this is easy (giving someone regeneration without enough successes to make it permanent require upkeep, but healing damage doesn't) but many other times the books are of little help.<br /> <br /> &quot;Curse&quot; is probably too broad of a concept to give a definite answer on. What Spheres and how many successes would matter a lot to whether or not it would be an effect that becomes its own &quot;thing&quot; that exists independently of the mage.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-15T18:25:25-07:00","id":1481207,"title":"","content":"I think Entropy 4 would be the best basis for laying an independent curse by manipulating a target's fate, though of course there are all sorts of long-term effects in the other Spheres that could be made to act in the manner of a curse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-17T05:31:59-07:00","id":1481284,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480882-does-a-curse-end-if-you-kill-him#post1480882\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, I was in this metaphysical discussion with a friend of mine, thinking about whether a curse cast by a mage ends which his demise or not.<br /> What is your guy's opinion on the matter?</div> </div> </div> </div>It entirely depends on the stipulations of the curse. An high dot curse with ritual casting and large expenditure of quintessence may persist for an indeterminate amount of time independently by the demise of its creator.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24992-haquim","name":"Haquim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24992_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-17T07:55:30-07:00","id":1481290,"title":"","content":"As far as I know, no general rule stipulates that a spell ends when the mage dies.<br /> <br /> One could argue that spells that require some sort of active upkeep would end on the mage's death. It's <i>common sense</i> that such effects would end. But, as said above, rules are of little help to determine if <i>that's the case</i>. You would need to judge on a case by case scenario.<br /> <br /> As a personal &quot;rule of thumb&quot;, I would suggest that if the spell it's something that requires concentration, or something the mage can control and/or &quot;end&quot; at will, if it doesn't seem to act as an &quot;independant&quot; spell, then it would end with the mage's death.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-17-2022, 10:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-17T11:29:17-07:00","id":1481309,"title":"","content":"Not to sound too redundant here, but I think Awakening solved this pretty well, and it's not hard to import the basic concepts it uses to Ascension to make this easier to run:<br /> <br /> 1) Lasting vs. long duration. In Awakening a lasting spell is a spell that only exists for an instant, but has &quot;lasting&quot; consequences. Healing and damaging spells are simple examples. The larger consideration is &quot;is there an ongoing spell you could try to diminish after it's cast or would you only be able to cast a new spell to the opposite effect?&quot; If you heal damage, the spell is over and someone that doesn't like that can only &quot;undo&quot; it by causing new damage. Barring Time shenanigans, they can't counterspell healed damage after it's been healed. If you teleport from A to B, same thing. You don't revert back to A after some duration, the spell ended the moment you moved locations. Nobody can counterspell you back to A, they could only cast a new spell to send you somewhere else. Etc.<br /> <br /> 2) Active vs. relinquished. Active spells are simple: they have a duration loner than instant, and aren't lasting. So the spell is ongoing in some fashion until it's duration (based on how the spells was first cast) would come to an end (running out of time, hitting a trigger, etc.). Every active spell counts towards a growing penalty to casting more spells (Ascension makes this a case-by-case thing with very little guidance on where the line should be). However, a mage can relinquish a spell at some significant but recoverable cost (such as a default of just paying a Willpower), it's not meant to be a huge deal, just to avoid spamming relinquishing low duration spells. This becomes a balancing act. Active spells are things your mage is at least partially thinking about, and thus grow into a pain to maintain a bunch of them, but while you retain active control over them... they're still your spells. You can try to stop people from messing with them, you can cancel them if you desire, etc. Relinquished spells stop counting towards your concentration penalty, and importantly they no longer depend on the mage's mental care, but the caster is no longer aware of what's going on to those spells because they let them go. If you release a ward protecting your sanctum, you don't get any warning if an enemy is trying to unweave it. If someone finds a magical item you released, you have no idea if they're trying to study it to profile your magical signature via resonance and so on.<br /> <br /> The big advantage is that it puts the choice in the hands of the caster rather than ST judgement. You can make a target feel like they're cursed, but you're really just following them around casting lasting spells on them to make their life suck, or you can curse a single action, or anything else that would keep the curse short and simple to avoid it becoming active. Or you make it a longer curse, and then pick between it being active (and thus dependent on you) or relinquished and it will go out and do what it was &quot;programmed&quot; to do.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-18T14:06:55-07:00","id":1481403,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480882-does-a-curse-end-if-you-kill-him?p=1481309#post1481309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not to sound too redundant here, but I think Awakening solved this pretty well, and it's not hard to import the basic concepts it uses to Ascension to make this easier to run:<br /> <br /> 1) Lasting vs. long duration. In Awakening a lasting spell is a spell that only exists for an instant, but has &quot;lasting&quot; consequences. Healing and damaging spells are simple examples. The larger consideration is &quot;is there an ongoing spell you could try to diminish after it's cast or would you only be able to cast a new spell to the opposite effect?&quot; If you heal damage, the spell is over and someone that doesn't like that can only &quot;undo&quot; it by causing new damage. Barring Time shenanigans, they can't counterspell healed damage after it's been healed. If you teleport from A to B, same thing. You don't revert back to A after some duration, the spell ended the moment you moved locations. Nobody can counterspell you back to A, they could only cast a new spell to send you somewhere else. Etc.<br /> <br /> 2) Active vs. relinquished. Active spells are simple: they have a duration loner than instant, and aren't lasting. So the spell is ongoing in some fashion until it's duration (based on how the spells was first cast) would come to an end (running out of time, hitting a trigger, etc.). Every active spell counts towards a growing penalty to casting more spells (Ascension makes this a case-by-case thing with very little guidance on where the line should be). However, a mage can relinquish a spell at some significant but recoverable cost (such as a default of just paying a Willpower), it's not meant to be a huge deal, just to avoid spamming relinquishing low duration spells. This becomes a balancing act. Active spells are things your mage is at least partially thinking about, and thus grow into a pain to maintain a bunch of them, but while you retain active control over them... they're still your spells. You can try to stop people from messing with them, you can cancel them if you desire, etc. Relinquished spells stop counting towards your concentration penalty, and importantly they no longer depend on the mage's mental care, but the caster is no longer aware of what's going on to those spells because they let them go. If you release a ward protecting your sanctum, you don't get any warning if an enemy is trying to unweave it. If someone finds a magical item you released, you have no idea if they're trying to study it to profile your magical signature via resonance and so on.<br /> <br /> The big advantage is that it puts the choice in the hands of the caster rather than ST judgement. You can make a target feel like they're cursed, but you're really just following them around casting lasting spells on them to make their life suck, or you can curse a single action, or anything else that would keep the curse short and simple to avoid it becoming active. Or you make it a longer curse, and then pick between it being active (and thus dependent on you) or relinquished and it will go out and do what it was &quot;programmed&quot; to do.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yes, that seems like the best approach. Does anybody know why I can't upload photos from my pc into the forum?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-18T14:15:52-07:00","id":1481404,"title":"","content":"I have no idea why specifically, but the forums have a ton of broken functions. I believe most people just upload to a free storage site, and then link from there, rather than trying to upload into the forum itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}